1
00:00:02,073 --> 00:00:11,628
Welcome to France's regions, part 1 of 1. First, I clear the chosen names for
a fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:12,293 --> 00:00:22,823
We work through 13 of the France regions the map names, once each: where it
sits, one fact worth keeping, and a hook to remember it by.

3
00:00:23,524 --> 00:00:26,635
Let's bring Hauts-de-France into view.

4
00:00:42,847 --> 00:00:54,701
Hauts-de-France runs along the northern border of France, above Île-de-France
and between Grand Est and Normandie, reaching the coast of the Channel with
Lille as its largest city.

5
00:00:55,450 --> 00:01:04,622
The Somme flows through the region, and the battlefields of 1916 with their
war cemeteries are the reason that most visitors come here.

6
00:01:05,289 --> 00:01:13,555
Remember the upper France whose name was voted on in 2016, and the older
Picardy below it named for the pike its soldiers carried.

7
00:01:14,362 --> 00:01:16,487
Let's bring Normandie into view.

8
00:01:32,159 --> 00:01:44,652
Normandie fills the north-west of the country, facing the Channel between
Brittany and the Belgian border, with Rouen as its largest city and
Mont-Saint-Michel standing on its edge.

9
00:01:45,337 --> 00:01:57,446
Vikings under Rollo were given this land in the year 911 and were speaking
French and calling themselves Normans within a century, and their descendants
took England in 1066.

10
00:01:58,105 --> 00:02:09,529
Remember the land of the Northmen whose speech fed into English, with the
landing beaches of 1944 along its shore.

11
00:02:10,382 --> 00:02:13,447
Let's bring Grand Est into view.

12
00:02:28,122 --> 00:02:41,555
Grand Est fills the north-eastern corner of France and reaches the border with
Germany, with Strasbourg as its largest city and the old lands of Alsace,
Lorraine and Champagne inside it.

13
00:02:42,213 --> 00:02:52,186
Champagne may only be called champagne if the wine comes from this region, a
name France has defended in courts all over the world for more than a century.

14
00:02:52,847 --> 00:03:02,309
Remember the great east that was only named in 2016, where the name champagne
is protected and Strasbourg keeps the parliament of Europe.

15
00:03:03,130 --> 00:03:05,765
Let's bring Île-de-France into view.

16
00:03:21,236 --> 00:03:33,345
Île-de-France wraps around Paris in the north of the country, the small piece
of land between the Seine, the Marne and the Oise, with Normandie and Grand
Est beyond it.

17
00:03:34,023 --> 00:03:44,391
It is the smallest region of France by area and still holds about a fifth of
the whole population, and every one of the high speed lines in the country
leaves from Paris.

18
00:03:45,059 --> 00:03:54,904
Remember the island that is not an island, the royal domain the kingdom grew
out from, with Versailles and every fast train leaving from its centre.

19
00:03:55,655 --> 00:03:58,035
Let's bring Bretagne into view.

20
00:04:06,349 --> 00:04:15,091
Bretagne is the peninsula at the far west of France, with Rennes as its
largest city and the Channel on one side and the Bay of Biscay on the other.

21
00:04:15,747 --> 00:04:25,256
Breton is a Celtic language much closer to Welsh than to French, and about two
hundred thousand people still speak it in every day conversation.

22
00:04:25,906 --> 00:04:34,300
Remember the western peninsula that took its name from Britons who crossed
from Britain, with more coastline than any other region of France.

23
00:04:35,027 --> 00:04:38,905
Let's bring Centre-Val de Loire into view.

24
00:04:44,796 --> 00:04:53,620
Centre-Val de Loire sits in the middle of France along the valley of the
Loire, with Tours as its largest city and the river running right through the
heart of it.

25
00:04:54,270 --> 00:05:03,186
The Loire is the longest river in France at one thousand and six kilometres,
and it is the last great river in Europe with no dam across its lower stretch.

26
00:05:03,839 --> 00:05:12,628
Remember the middle of the country where the long river passes the great
châteaux of Chambord and Chenonceau, kept as a world heritage site.

27
00:05:13,357 --> 00:05:16,596
Let's bring Pays de la Loire into view.

28
00:05:25,032 --> 00:05:35,226
Pays de la Loire runs down the west side of France from Normandie to the
Atlantic, with Nantes as its largest city and the river Loire only clipping
its southern half.

29
00:05:35,884 --> 00:05:47,691
The region was put together in 1955 out of several older provinces, and Nantes
was the historic capital of Brittany, which many Bretons still treat as the
capital to this day.

30
00:05:48,356 --> 00:05:58,631
Remember the awkward name for lands the Loire only grazes, with the twenty
four hour race at Le Mans running on ordinary public roads.

31
00:05:59,376 --> 00:06:02,522
Let's bring Bourgogne-Franche-Comté into view.

32
00:06:09,750 --> 00:06:18,539
Bourgogne-Franche-Comté lies in eastern France between Paris and the Alps,
with Dijon as its largest city and no sea anywhere in sight.

33
00:06:19,206 --> 00:06:27,217
The Côte d’Or hillside inside it produces the most expensive wine on earth by
the hectare, and some of those plots are smaller than a tennis court.

34
00:06:27,876 --> 00:06:36,061
Remember the free county and the land of the Burgundians, whose dukes were so
rich in the fifteenth century that they behaved like a rival kingdom.

35
00:06:36,874 --> 00:06:40,322
Let's bring Auvergne-Rhône-Alpes into view.

36
00:06:48,633 --> 00:07:01,508
Auvergne-Rhône-Alpes covers the mountainous centre and east of France, from
the volcanic hills of the Massif Central up to the Alps, with Lyon as its
largest city.

37
00:07:02,163 --> 00:07:15,596
The Chaîne des Puys here is a chain of about eighty extinct volcanoes and a
world heritage site, and Mont Blanc stands on the border of the region at four
thousand eight hundred and eight metres.

38
00:07:16,247 --> 00:07:24,816
Remember the land of the Arverni, whose chief Vercingetorix fought Caesar,
with the highest summit in the Alps on its edge.

39
00:07:25,502 --> 00:07:30,529
Let's bring Nouvelle-Aquitaine into view.

40
00:07:33,413 --> 00:07:45,906
Nouvelle-Aquitaine is the largest region in France, bigger than Austria,
stretching from the Atlantic coast up to the Loire and down to the Pyrenees,
with Bordeaux in the middle.

41
00:07:46,558 --> 00:07:56,858
Eleanor of Aquitaine carried the land to the English crown by her marriage in
1152, and it stayed under English rule for the three hundred years that
followed.

42
00:07:57,519 --> 00:08:05,112
Remember the new Aquitaine, the land of waters, where a duchess married an
English king and took the whole region across the Channel.

43
00:08:05,821 --> 00:08:09,234
Let's bring Provence-Alpes-Côte-d'Azur into view.

44
00:08:14,554 --> 00:08:25,386
Provence-Alpes-Côte-d’Azur fills the south-eastern corner of France on the
Mediterranean, with Marseille as its largest city and the Alps along its
northern edge.

45
00:08:26,041 --> 00:08:36,536
The Romans made this their first province outside Italy and called it
provincia, and because it mattered so much to them, Provence became their word
for the whole region.

46
00:08:37,199 --> 00:08:48,414
Remember the province whose coast was named for the colour of the sea, with
the Roman arena at Arles and the theatre at Orange among the finest outside
Italy.

47
00:08:49,154 --> 00:08:51,534
Let's bring Occitanie into view.

48
00:09:05,681 --> 00:09:14,725
Occitanie runs across the south of France from the Atlantic to the
Mediterranean, with Toulouse as its largest city and the Pyrenees along its
southern border.

49
00:09:15,390 --> 00:09:27,627
The region took its name in 2016 from the langue d’oc, the southern tongue in
which yes was oc, and the troubadours wrote the earliest poetry of Europe in a
living language in it.

50
00:09:28,277 --> 00:09:36,973
Remember the tongue of the troubadours, with the double walls of Carcassonne
and the canal dug to join the Atlantic to the Mediterranean.

51
00:09:37,909 --> 00:09:40,080
Let's bring Corse into view.

52
00:09:54,636 --> 00:10:04,191
Corse is the island in the Mediterranean off the south-east of France, with
Ajaccio as its largest city and no land border with the mainland.

53
00:10:04,846 --> 00:10:15,597
Genoa sold the island to France in 1768, and Napoleon was born in Ajaccio a
year later, so the Corsican became French by a margin of only months.

54
00:10:16,254 --> 00:10:24,823
Remember the island the Greeks called the most beautiful, with the trail along
its spine rated the hardest long walk in Europe.

55
00:10:40,182 --> 00:11:01,926
13 regions of France, one for each target of the map: Hauts-de-France,
Normandie, Grand Est, Île-de-France, Bretagne, Centre-Val de Loire, Pays de la
Loire, Bourgogne-Franche-Comté, Auvergne-Rhône-Alpes, Nouvelle-Aquitaine,
Provence-Alpes-Côte-d'Azur, Occitanie and Corse.

56
00:11:02,588 --> 00:11:13,165
Remember the shape of France from the inside: the regions nest around each
other, and every name on the map belongs somewhere in this one country.
